Brief Review of Lecture: Control Flow
If / Else = Conditional Behavior
if (x &gt; 0) {
  print("positive")
} else {
  print("not positive")
}
Vectorized If/Else statements are also possible:

x = 0
ifelse(test = x &lt; 0, yes = "is positive :)", no = "is negative :(")



[1] "is negative :("


For loops = repeat over items
for (i in 1:5) {
  print(i^2)
}

Brief Review of Lecture: Functions

function_name &lt;- function(arg1, arg2, ...) {
  # code to execute
  result &lt;- arg1 + arg2  # example operation
  return(result)         # optional, returns result
}

if-else
“if else” statement: executes one block of code if a condition is met and a different block of code if it is not met.


x &lt;- rnorm(1)

if (x &gt; 0) {
  print("x is positive :)")
} else {
  print("x is negative :(")
}


Similarly, run it multiple times and annotate code with different colors: if-then syntax, logical condition, and what to do.

Nested ifs


x &lt;- rnorm(1)

if (x &gt; 0) {
  print("x is positive :)")
} else if (x &lt; 0) {
  print("x is negative :(")
} else {
  print("x is zero :|")
}


Run it multiple times, then get the zero to toggle using x &lt;- 0

